    "answer": {
      "short": "The intersections lie strictly between $P=(\\tfrac12,\\tfrac32)$ and $Q=(\\tfrac32,\\tfrac12)$ on the line $y=2-x$. Hence $AB<PQ=\\sqrt2$.\n\nUse reflection in $y=x$ to obtain the second intersection from the first.",
      "worked": "Let $A$ be the intersection with $y=2^x$. At $x=\\tfrac12$, the exponential has height $\\sqrt2<\\tfrac32$, while the line has height $\\tfrac32$. At $x=1$, the exponential has height $2>1$, the height of the line. Since the exponential increases and the line decreases, their intersection satisfies $\\tfrac12<x_A<1$.\n\nThe two curves are reflections in $y=x$, and reflection in $y=x$ leaves the line $x+y=2$ unchanged. Thus $B$ is the reflection of $A$, so $1<x_B<\\tfrac32$. Both intersections therefore lie strictly inside the segment joining $P=(\\tfrac12,\\tfrac32)$ to $Q=(\\tfrac32,\\tfrac12)$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}PQ&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\tfrac32-\\tfrac12\\right)^2+\\left(\\tfrac12-\\tfrac32\\right)^2}\\\\PQ&=\\sqrt2.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore $AB<\\sqrt2$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
